.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt LDAPSTNS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ldapstns
.Nd serve an STNS directory to macOS Open Directory over LDAPv3
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dnv
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
presents the users and groups of an
.Lk https://stns.jp/ STNS
API server as a read-only LDAPv3 directory on the loopback interface, so that
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
can resolve them with the
.Pa ldap.bundle
module it already ships.
.Pp
macOS has nothing resembling
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5 ,
and it has no way to add a directory module either: every module in
.Pa /System/Library/OpenDirectory/Modules
is an Apple-signed bundle in a directory System Integrity Protection will not
allow anything to be added to.
What is left is to speak a protocol one of the shipped modules already
understands, which is what
.Nm
does.
.Pp
The daemon holds the whole directory in memory and refreshes it on a timer.
A search is answered out of that snapshot by a child process forked for the
connection, so no client ever waits on an HTTP round trip, several clients are
answered at once, and the only code an untrusted peer can reach can neither
modify the directory nor outlive the connection.
If a refresh fails, the previous snapshot is kept and served: an API server
that is briefly unreachable must not empty the directory out from under
everybody logged in.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not detach from the terminal
.Pq which is the default in any case, see below
and copy every log line to standard error.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read
.Ar file
instead of the default configuration file.
.It Fl n
Read the configuration, print what was made of it, and exit.
The exit status is zero only if the configuration is one the daemon would
agree to start with.
.It Fl v
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss Starting it
.Nm
does not put itself into the background.
On macOS, starting and supervising a service is
.Xr launchd 8 Ns 's
job, and a program that forks itself away takes that job from it: launchd would
watch the process that exited immediately rather than the one doing the work.
A job description is installed as
.Pa /usr/local/share/ldapstns/jp.stns.ldapstns.plist ;
put it in place and load it with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo cp /usr/local/share/ldapstns/jp.stns.ldapstns.plist /Library/LaunchDaemons/
sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/jp.stns.ldapstns.plist
.Ed
.Pp
The job uses launchd socket activation: launchd binds port 389 itself, as root,
before the job starts, and hands the descriptor over.
That is why the plist can name an unprivileged
.Sy UserName
and the daemon never holds root at all.
Started by hand instead,
.Nm
binds its own socket and then drops to the user named by
.Sy user
in the configuration file.
.Ss Pointing Open Directory at it
Add the node and put it in the search policy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo dsconfigldap -a 127.0.0.1
.Ed
.Pp
No search base, no mapping and no schema file need to be given.
.Pp
macOS has two mappings, in
.Pa /System/Library/OpenDirectory/Mappings :
one for an Open Directory server, which looks for users under
.Ql cn=users
with five object classes combined with AND, and one for RFC 2307, which looks
anywhere under the naming context for three combined with OR.
Which of them is used is not something
.Xr dsconfigldap 8
can be told - it probes the server and decides, and it decides Open Directory.
.Nm
therefore serves the Open Directory layout, whose entries carry every class
either mapping asks for and so satisfy both.
.Pp
Both leave the search base as
.Qq %! ,
meaning it is to be read from the root DSE - and
.Nm
advertises the configured suffix there as
.Sy namingContexts .
.Pp
Check the result without involving the rest of the system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dscl /LDAPv3/127.0.0.1 -read /Users/alice
dscl /LDAPv3/127.0.0.1 -list /Groups
.Ed
.Pp
and then through the search policy, which is what everything else on the
machine uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
id alice
dscacheutil -q user -a name alice
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr opendirectoryd 8
caches aggressively.
After changing anything, flush it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo dscacheutil -flushcache
sudo killall -HUP opendirectoryd
.Ed
.Pp
To undo the whole thing,
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo dsconfigldap -r 127.0.0.1
.Ed
.Ss What is served
Users appear as
.Ql uid=<name>,cn=users,<suffix>
with the object classes
.Sy posixAccount ,
.Sy inetOrgPerson
and
.Sy shadowAccount ,
carrying
.Sy uid ,
.Sy uidNumber ,
.Sy gidNumber ,
.Sy cn ,
.Sy sn ,
.Sy gecos ,
.Sy description ,
.Sy homeDirectory ,
.Sy loginShell
and, where the directory holds any,
.Sy sshPublicKey .
.Pp
Groups appear as
.Ql cn=<name>,cn=groups,<suffix>
with the object class
.Sy posixGroup ,
carrying
.Sy cn ,
.Sy gidNumber
and one
.Sy memberUid
per member.
.Pp
.Sy GeneratedUID
is served as
.Sy apple-generateduid ,
in the scheme macOS uses for its own local records:
FFFFEEEE-DDDD-CCCC-BBBB-AAAA followed by the uid in hexadecimal for a user,
ABCDEFAB-CDEF-ABCD-EFAB-CDEF followed by the gid for a group.
The Open Directory mapping reads it from there; the RFC 2307 mapping does not
map it and macOS derives the same value for itself.
Either way a user has the same UUID on every machine that resolves them here,
and the same one they would have had as a local account.
.Ss What is not served
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Password hashes
.Sy userPassword
is withheld unless
.Sy expose_password
is set, and that cannot be set without a
.Sy bind_dn ;
see
.Xr ldapstns.conf 5 .
A loopback socket is reachable by every process on the machine, so serving
hashes to an anonymous client would amount to a world-readable shadow file.
Note also that macOS cannot verify the SHA-512 hashes
.Pq Li $6$ Ns ...
most STNS deployments store, so turning it on is rarely the answer it looks
like.
Authenticating with SSH keys, through
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
is the path that works.
.It Writes
Add, modify, delete, modifyDN and compare are not implemented.
An operation
.Nm
does not recognise is answered with a notice of disconnection.
.It StartTLS, and every other extended operation
Refused with
.Sy unwillingToPerform .
The daemon listens on the loopback interface, where there is no network for a
transport to be secured against, and a half-implemented negotiation would be
worse than an honest refusal.
.It SASL
Refused with
.Sy authMethodNotSupported .
Simple bind only.
.El
.Ss SSH keys
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
is installed alongside and is unrelated to any of the above -
.Xr sshd 8
runs a command and reads its output, which needs nothing from Open Directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
AuthorizedKeysCommand     /usr/local/bin/stns-key-wrapper
AuthorizedKeysCommandUser nobody
.Ed
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv SIGHUP
Re-read both configuration files and refresh the directory.
A change to
.Sy listen
or
.Sy port
is reported and not applied; restart for those.
If
.Pa stns.conf
can no longer be read - it is read at startup while still root, and may well
be mode 600 - the settings already in memory are kept.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/ldapstns.conf
This daemon's settings.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration, shared with
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 .
.It Pa /usr/local/share/ldapstns/jp.stns.ldapstns.plist
The launchd job description, staged for the administrator to install.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
In particular
.Nm
exits rather than starting up with an empty directory when the very first
refresh fails.
Answering
.Qq no such user
for everybody is a worse outcome than not running, and launchd will try again.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a configuration before installing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ldapstns -n -f ./ldapstns.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Run it in the foreground with everything on the terminal, on a port that needs
no privileges:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ldapstns -d -f ./ldapstns.conf
ldapsearch -x -LLL -H ldap://127.0.0.1:11389 -b dc=stns "(uid=alice)"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dscacheutil 1 ,
.Xr dscl 1 ,
.Xr ldapsearch 1 ,
.Xr odutil 1 ,
.Xr launchd.plist 5 ,
.Xr ldapstns.conf 5 ,
.Xr dsconfigldap 8 ,
.Xr opendirectoryd 8 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
.Pp
.Lk https://stns.jp/ "STNS"
.Pp
.Pa /System/Library/OpenDirectory/Mappings/RFC2307.plist ,
which is the specification the attributes above are chosen to meet.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
